        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Why does SNARC deserve its own place in the history of artificial intelligence?",
            "sourceIds": [],
            "claimIds": [],
            "contentOrigin": "editorial-hook"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "SNARC was an early stochastic neural analog reinforcement calculator built by Marvin Minsky and Dean Edmonds. It used simulated synapses and reward-driven behavior to model a rat learning a maze.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-snarc-history-overview"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.historical-background"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "A simulated rat receives reinforcement and changes path preferences through a maze.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-snarc-history-overview"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.core-idea"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Experts generally treat SNARC as historically important early neural hardware, even though it was not a scalable general AI system. Its long-term legacy is the early demonstration that learning, reward, and neural-style machinery could be combined in a physical device.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-snarc-history-overview"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.long-term-legacy"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "As today's systems grow larger, which lessons from SNARC still matter, and which limits deserve to be questioned again?",
